consiSt("al",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Alabama","d":"Nine members (a chief justice and eight associate justices sign its orders). Hears civil appeals not within the Court of Civil Appeals’ exclusive jurisdiction, reviews both intermediate courts by discretionary certiorari, decides permissive interlocutory appeals (Ala. R. App. P. 5) and mandamus petitions in its cases, answers certified questions from federal courts, and makes all court rules. May transfer (“pour over”) civil appeals to the Court of Civil Appeals except constitutional, novel statewide-impact, utility-rate, bond-validation, and bar-discipline cases (Ala. Code § 12-2-7)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Civil Appeals","d":"Exclusive appellate jurisdiction of civil cases with $50,000 or less involved (exclusive of interest and costs), domestic-relations and workers’ compensation appeals, and appeals from administrative agencies other than the Public Service Commission (Ala. Code § 12-3-10)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Criminal Appeals","d":"Hears criminal appeals. In criminal cases an application for rehearing there is a prerequisite to Supreme Court certiorari review (Ala. R. App. P. 40(d)(1))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit; jury trials in civil cases (struck juries of 12 under Ala. R. Civ. P. 47). Governed by the Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure; local rules are abolished (Ala. R. Civ. P. 83)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Courts","d":"County-level courts of limited civil jurisdiction with no jury trials. Exclusive small-claims jurisdiction up to $6,000 (Ala. Code § 12-12-31). The civil rules apply as modified by each rule’s “(dc)” paragraph (shorter deadlines, discovery only by leave or agreement). Final judgments are appealed to circuit court for trial de novo (Ala. Code § 12-12-71)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Probate and Municipal Courts","d":"Probate courts handle estates and other statutory matters, with the civil rules applying where appropriate (Ala. R. Civ. P. 1(a)); appeals from probate final judgments lie under Ala. Code § 12-22-2. Municipal courts handle ordinance violations. Their own rules were not collected."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Code of Alabama","d":"A search of the official Code of Alabama on the Legislature’s ALISON site (Nov. 10, 2026) for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public expression,” “public participation,” and “special motion” found no special motion to dismiss or strike, discovery stay, or fee-shifting procedure for claims based on speech or petitioning. A full-text search of bills in all sessions on ALISON (“SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuits,” “public expression,” “matter of public concern,” “right of free speech”) surfaced no anti-SLAPP bill among the top results; that search ranks by relevance and only the first results were reviewed, so an introduced bill using other wording could have been missed. Defendants rely on ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) and Rule 56 motions.","c":"Code of Alabama 1975 (ALISON search, Nov. 10, 2026); Ala. R. Civ. P. 12, 56","v":true},{"t":"Related protection that does exist: libel retraction demand","d":"Punitive damages for libel require proof that the defendant published with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard, and that the plaintiff made a written demand for a public retraction at least 5 days before suit and the defendant failed to publish a full and fair retraction within 5 days. This limits damages; it is not an early-dismissal procedure.","c":"Ala. Code § 6-5-186","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: who is eligible","d":"A lawyer in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction, of good moral character and familiar with Alabama’s ethics and practice, may appear pro hac vice in a particular case before any Alabama court or state administrative agency. Not eligible: lawyers who live in Alabama, are regularly employed in Alabama, or regularly engage in substantial business or professional activities in Alabama. Admission is discretionary, and the court may examine the lawyer on Alabama ethics and revoke permission.","c":"Rules Governing Admission to the Ala. State Bar, Rule VII.A, VII.B, VII.E","v":true},{"t":"Verified application, $300 fee, and the Bar’s statement first","d":"File a verified application (on the State Bar’s form) with the court or agency, with proof of service by mail on the Alabama State Bar in Montgomery or by e-mail to PHV@alabar.org, plus a nonrefundable $300 fee to the Bar. If no defendant has appeared yet, serve each defendant too. The Bar then files a statement listing prior applications by you and your firm; no application may be granted before that statement is filed. The application lists every Alabama pro hac vice application by you or your firm in the past 3 years. Local counsel must mail or e-mail the order granting or denying admission to the Bar. Rule VII.D–E was amended effective Oct. 13, 2026; the posted rule reflects that order.","c":"Rule VII.D, VII.E (as amended eff. Oct. 13, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must appear in person and is jointly liable","d":"You must associate an Alabama State Bar member in good standing whose name appears on every filing. Local counsel must personally appear and participate in all pretrial conferences, hearings, trials, and other open-court proceedings unless the court excuses it, and takes on joint and several responsibility to the client, opponents, and the court. Before the application is granted, local counsel must appear as attorney of record or consent in writing. If local counsel is suspended or disbarred, you must associate new counsel and file a verified notice before proceeding.","c":"Rule VII.C, VII.E, VII.I","v":true},{"t":"Repeat appearances: 5 cases in 12 months triggers scrutiny","d":"Repeated appearances by a lawyer or firm, absent special circumstances, are cause for denial. If you have appeared pro hac vice in 5 cases in the preceding 12 months, the court must examine you for good cause based on the client’s interests, such as a complex specialty, a long-standing client relationship, no local expertise, questions of your home jurisdiction’s law, or extensive discovery there.","c":"Rule VII.E","v":true},{"t":"Annual certification by March 1 ($100 per case)","d":"Lawyers admitted on or after Oct. 1, 2020 must submit an annual certification for each case, received by the State Bar by March 1, with a nonrefundable $100 fee, showing continued compliance or that you are no longer counsel of record.","c":"Rule VII.G","v":true},{"t":"Appeals and transfers","d":"Admission carries over when the case is transferred or appealed, but in the appellate court you must file proof of the earlier admission and certify that it has not been rescinded. If the appeal is your first appearance, move for admission in the appellate court with a certificate of good standing; local counsel is required (it may be waived for good cause) but need not actively participate, and you must designate lead counsel for service. Admission in a court of appeals carries to the Supreme Court in the same matter.","c":"Rule VII.E, VII.F","v":true},{"t":"Filings before admission are stricken; violation is unauthorized practice","d":"Clerks may not accept filings from a lawyer who has not complied, and noncompliant filings are stricken on motion or sua sponte. Permission ends automatically on suspension or disbarment anywhere, and you must report discipline elsewhere to the Alabama court. Violating Rule VII is deemed the unauthorized practice of law. Exceptions: pro bono counsel in capital or indigent postconviction cases (no fee, no local counsel), lawyers appearing for themselves, and lawyers for the U.S. government.","c":"Rule VII.A, VII.I, VII.J, VII.K","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","d":"Subject to Rule VII for court appearances, a lawyer admitted and in good standing elsewhere may provide temporary transactional, counseling, or other nonlitigation services reasonably related to the home-state practice, and services related to arbitration, mediation, or other ADR held in Alabama or elsewhere. No office or permanent presence in Alabama and no holding out as admitted here. Note: Rule 5.5(b)(3) cross-refers to house counsel under “Rule IX,” but the posted admission rules place authorized house counsel in Rule VIII.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 5.5(b)–(e); Rules Governing Admission, Rule VIII","v":true},{"t":"Authorized house counsel","d":"A lawyer licensed elsewhere who is exclusively employed by a business organization located in or relocating to Alabama may register as authorized house counsel without the bar exam, agreeing to the Alabama Rules of Professional Conduct and Rules of Disciplinary Procedure.","c":"Rules Governing Admission, Rule VIII.A–B","v":true},{"t":"Admission without examination (reciprocity)","d":"Requires admission elsewhere, active practice in 5 of the 6 years before applying, reciprocity from your principal-practice or domicile jurisdiction, good standing everywhere, no pending discipline, no failed Alabama bar exam within 10 years, an MPRE score of 75 or more, and Alabama residence or a certified intention to do at least 75% of your practice in Alabama from an Alabama office. Fee $875 plus NCBE character-investigation costs. Amendments effective Jan. 1, 2027 change the law-school requirement to schools approved under amended Rule IV.B (reducing reliance on ABA accreditation).","c":"Rules Governing Admission, Rule III.A (and order of Apr. 30, 2026, eff. Jan. 1, 2027); Fee Schedule item (4)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting","d":"A lawyer may provide limited-scope representation in a court proceeding by filing and serving a notice of limited-scope representation at or before the start of the proceeding, and withdraws without leave by filing a notice of completion. A lawyer who only drafts papers for a self-represented person need not sign them but must include the certification that the document was prepared with the assistance of a licensed Alabama lawyer under Rule 1.2(c). Opposing counsel may communicate directly with the client on matters outside a noticed limited scope. These provisions speak of Alabama lawyers; whether a pro hac vice lawyer may use them is not addressed.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 87, 11(b)(2); Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.2(c), 4.2(b)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access","d":"Appellate courts: attorneys may e-file after registering; licensed Alabama lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers authorized in the appellate proceeding may register (pro se parties may not e-file). Trial courts: papers filed electronically “in accordance with an order or rules of the Supreme Court” count as filed, and service on registered users through the system is complete on sending; the trial-court e-filing (AlaFile) registration rules and whether pro hac vice lawyers can register were not collected.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 57; Ala. R. Civ. P. 5(b)(2)(E), 5(d)(1)","v":false}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Alabama Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Adopted by the Supreme Court of Alabama; enforced through the Alabama Rules of Disciplinary Procedure. Recent changes: Rule 1.15 (trust accounts) amended effective Feb. 15, 2025; Rules 7.1, 7.2, 7.3, and 8.5 (advertising, solicitation, jurisdiction) amended effective Jan. 1, 2026. Several rules keep older ABA Model Rule language, as noted below.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond.; orders of Dec. 23, 2024 and May 13, 2025","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: much narrower exceptions (Rule 1.6)","d":"Disclosure is allowed only to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm, and for the lawyer’s own claims and defenses. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.6(b), there are no exceptions for client crimes or frauds causing financial injury, securing ethics advice, complying with other law or a court order, or detecting conflicts when changing firms.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.6","v":true},{"t":"Fees: “clearly excessive” standard; contingency rules (Rule 1.5)","d":"The standard is a “clearly excessive” fee (the ABA uses “unreasonable”), and the factors include whether there is a written fee agreement signed by the client. Contingency agreements must be in writing, state the percentages on settlement, trial, or appeal and how expenses are handled, and end with a written closing statement. No contingent fees in divorce, alimony, or support matters or for criminal defendants.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.5(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Financial assistance to clients allowed in emergencies (Rule 1.8(e))","d":"Besides advancing costs, a lawyer may advance or guarantee emergency financial assistance to a litigation client if repayment is not contingent on the outcome and no assistance was promised before the lawyer was hired. In percentage-fee cases, the lawyer may pay costs from the lawyer’s own account and recover them from the proceeds. The ABA rule has no general emergency-assistance exception.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.8(e)(3)–(4)","v":true},{"t":"No screening cure for lateral hires (Rule 1.10)","d":"Alabama’s imputation rule disqualifies a firm when an incoming lawyer (or the lawyer’s former firm) represented an adverse client in the same or a substantially related matter and the lawyer has material confidential information. The rule text contains no screening provision like ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2); the affected client can waive under Rule 1.7. Imputation also covers Rule 2.2 (intermediary), a rule the ABA deleted.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.10(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Broad misconduct catch-all; no anti-harassment rule (Rule 8.4)","d":"Rule 8.4(g) makes it misconduct to “engage in any other conduct that adversely reflects on [the lawyer’s] fitness to practice law.” Alabama has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Solicitation waiting periods (Rule 7.3)","d":"Even where live solicitation is not otherwise barred, a lawyer may not solicit about a personal-injury or wrongful-death claim within 21 days after the accident or disaster; about a pending civil or administrative proceeding until service on the prospective client has been obtained and filed; or about a pending criminal case until 7 days after service of the warrant or information. The ABA rule has no such waiting periods.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 7.3(b)(1)–(3) (as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Disciplinary jurisdiction over visiting lawyers (Rule 8.5)","d":"Lawyers not admitted in Alabama are subject to Alabama discipline if they provide or offer legal services here, and pro hac vice lawyers also submit to the courts and the State Bar’s disciplinary bodies and authorize the Bar to report discipline to every licensing jurisdiction. Amended Rule 8.5 (eff. Jan. 1, 2026) contains no choice-of-law paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.5(b); its comment leaves conflicts to “principles of conflict of laws.”","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 8.5; Rules Governing Admission, Rule VII.B","v":true}],"style":"State of Alabama","home":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The 90-day deemed denial restarts your appeal clock without warning","d":"A Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 motion not ruled on within 90 days is denied by operation of law, and the 42-day appeal period runs from that date, even if the judge later holds a hearing or rules. Only express consent of all parties on the record, or an appellate-court extension, stops it.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1; Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3)"},{"t":"42 days to appeal, 14 for injunctions; no appellate extension","d":"The notice of appeal is due 42 days after entry (not 30), but only 14 days for interlocutory injunction, receivership, and similar orders. A late notice must be dismissed, and appellate courts cannot extend the time; the trial court may add up to 30 days only for failure to learn of the entry.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1), 2(a)(1), 2(b), 26(b); Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d)"},{"t":"Two different weekend rules for computing time","d":"Under the civil rules, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded for periods under 11 days; under the appellate rules, only for periods under 7 days. Three days are added after service by mail or through the e-filing system in the trial court.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 6(a)(2), 6(d); Ala. R. App. P. 26(a)"},{"t":"Plaintiff’s claims against a third-party defendant are compulsory","d":"If a defendant impleads a third party, the plaintiff’s failure to assert claims against that third-party defendant arising from the same transaction has the same effect as failing to plead a compulsory counterclaim.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(3)"},{"t":"Two suits for the same cause: the first one bars the second","d":"A plaintiff may not prosecute two actions in Alabama courts at the same time for the same cause against the same party; the pendency of the earlier action is a defense to the later one, and simultaneous actions force an election.","c":"Ala. Code § 6-5-440"},{"t":"Local counsel must physically attend every hearing","d":"Pro hac vice local counsel is not a name on the pleadings: local counsel must personally appear at all pretrial conferences, hearings, and trials unless excused, and shares joint and several responsibility. Filings made before you comply with Rule VII are stricken, and the Bar’s statement on your prior applications must be filed before the court can grant admission.","c":"Rules Governing Admission, Rule VII.C, VII.D, VII.K"},{"t":"Jury demand within 30 days of the last pleading","d":"Serve a written jury demand no later than 30 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (10 days in an appeal to circuit court), and file it. Failure to demand, or failing to appear at trial, waives the jury.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)"},{"t":"Supersedeas bond is 125% to 150% of the judgment","d":"To stay a money judgment pending appeal, the bond must be 150% of a judgment of $10,000 or less, or 125% of a larger one, approved by the trial-court clerk. The automatic stay lasts only 30 days after entry.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 8(a)(1); Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a)"},{"t":"Want the Supreme Court to see your facts? Put them in the rehearing application","d":"If the Court of Civil Appeals misstated the facts or affirmed without opinion, your additional or corrected facts must be presented in the application for rehearing in that court and copied verbatim into the certiorari petition. Certiorari is due 14 days after the decision (or the rehearing ruling) and cannot be extended.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 39(c), 39(d)(5), 40(e)"},{"t":"Interlocutory permissive appeal only in Supreme Court cases","d":"A Rule 5 permissive appeal is available only in civil cases within the Supreme Court’s original appellate jurisdiction, needs a trial-judge certification (presumptively within 28 days) stating the controlling question, and the petition is due 14 days after certification. Cases within the Court of Civil Appeals’ jurisdiction (e.g., $50,000 or less, domestic relations) cannot use it.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)"},{"t":"Wrongful death: 2 years and county limits","d":"Alabama wrongful-death actions belong to the personal representative, must be filed within 2 years after death, and only in a county where the decedent could have sued under the venue statutes (§§ 6-3-2, 6-3-7).","c":"Ala. Code § 6-5-410(a), (d), (e)"},{"t":"Confidentiality exceptions are narrower than you are used to","d":"Alabama Rule 1.6 permits disclosure only to prevent a client crime likely to cause imminent death or substantial bodily harm and for the lawyer’s own claims and defenses; there is no financial-fraud, ethics-advice, or court-order exception in the rule text.","c":"Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.6(b)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory strike of a judge; recusal for cause","d":"Alabama has no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge in the collected rules or statutes. Recusal is for cause under Canon 3C of the Canons of Judicial Ethics: a judge should disqualify where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including personal bias, prior work as a lawyer in the matter, a financial interest of the judge, spouse, or minor child, or a relative within the fourth degree who is a party, has an interest, or is a likely material witness. For the financial-interest and relationship grounds, the parties and lawyers may all agree in writing, independently of the judge, to remit the disqualification (Canon 3D). By statute, no judge may sit where interested, related to a party within the fourth degree, or formerly of counsel, unless the parties consent of record.","c":"Ala. Canons of Jud. Ethics 3C, 3D; Ala. Code § 12-1-12","v":true},{"t":"Campaign-contribution recusal with a 30-day appeal","d":"In civil cases a judge must recuse if a party’s substantial campaign contribution or electioneering communication in the judge’s last election would make a reasonable person perceive impaired impartiality, or creates a serious, objective probability of bias. Recusal is presumed when a party’s direct contribution exceeded 10% (appellate), 15% (circuit), or 25% (district) of the judge’s total contributions for the cycle. “Party” includes 5% owners, affiliates, the party’s lawyer, and that lawyer’s colleagues. Denial of the motion is appealable within 30 days, and the case is stayed if the threshold is met.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-24-3","v":true},{"t":"Contempt involving a personal attack on the judge","d":"If alleged contempt involves gross disrespect toward or a personal attack on the judge, or the judge’s own conduct is entangled with it, the matter must be referred to another judge unless it is a direct contempt requiring prompt summary punishment.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 70A(f)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Alabama Constitution free-speech provisions: not established here","d":"The text of the Alabama Constitution’s free-speech and petition provisions, and whether Alabama courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment, was not collected. Do not argue an independent state standard without checking the constitution and case law.","c":"Ala. Const. (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Libel: retraction demand before suit for punitive damages","d":"No punitive damages for libel unless the plaintiff proves knowing or reckless falsity and made a written retraction demand at least 5 days before suing that went unanswered by a full and fair retraction within 5 days.","c":"Ala. Code § 6-5-186","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Fictitious-party pleading","d":"Alabama lets a plaintiff sue an unknown defendant by a fictitious name and later substitute the true name, with relation back under principles of fictitious-party practice. Fictitious defendants still unnamed 75 days after a venue-transfer motion are ignored for venue.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 9(h), 15(c)(4), 82(d)(4)","v":true},{"t":"No local rules","d":"All local court rules were abolished effective April 14, 1992, and none are permitted.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 83","v":true},{"t":"Struck juries","d":"Civil juries are selected by alternately striking from a list of at least 24 qualified jurors until 12 remain, the party demanding the jury striking first; with multiple parties the plaintiff side receives half of all strikes.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 47(b), (c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Alabama Rules of Appellate Procedure, rule-by-rule texts posted by the Supreme Court of Alabama / State Law Library (judicial.alabama.gov/library/AppellateProcedure), reflecting amendments through the order of Apr. 16, 2026 (Rule 29(c)–(d), eff. June 1, 2026) and Mar. 31, 2026 (Rules 53(c), 54(c)); Rule 4 as amended eff. Oct. 1, 2025. Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure 4(f), 54(b), 58, 59, 59.1, 62, 77(d) (same source). Ala. Code §§ 12-2-7, 12-3-10, 12-12-71, 12-22-2, 12-24-3 (ALISON Code of Alabama). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Alabama: 42 days, two intermediate courts split by subject and amount, and a 90-day deemed-denial trap","d":"Civil appeals from circuit and probate courts go by notice of appeal, filed in the trial court within 42 days of entry of judgment. The Supreme Court of Alabama takes general civil appeals over $50,000; the Court of Civil Appeals has exclusive jurisdiction of civil cases of $50,000 or less (exclusive of interest and costs), domestic-relations and workers’ compensation appeals, and most agency appeals (Ala. Code § 12-3-10). The Supreme Court may also transfer (“pour over”) civil appeals within its own jurisdiction to the Court of Civil Appeals, except cases raising substantial constitutional questions or novel issues of statewide impact, utility-rate cases, bond validations, and bar discipline (§ 12-2-7(6)). Supreme Court review of the Court of Civil Appeals is by discretionary certiorari on limited grounds.\nTimeliness of the notice of appeal is jurisdictional: an untimely appeal “shall be dismissed,” and no appellate court may extend the time (Rules 2(a)(1), 2(b), 26(b)). Post-judgment motions under Civil Rules 50, 52, 55, and 59 suspend the appeal clock, but a motion not ruled on within 90 days is denied by operation of law (Civil Rule 59.1), and the 42 days then run from that date.\nMost appellate deadlines are written in multiples of 7 days (e.g., “14 days (2 weeks)”). District-court civil judgments go to circuit court for trial de novo (§ 12-12-71), not to an appellate court."},"groups":[["Which court and what is appealable",[{"t":"Supreme Court vs. Court of Civil Appeals","d":"The Court of Civil Appeals has exclusive appellate jurisdiction of civil cases where the amount involved does not exceed $50,000 (the recovery below if any, otherwise the amount claimed), all agency appeals except from the Public Service Commission, workers’ compensation, and domestic-relations cases (annulment, divorce, adoption, custody). Other civil appeals go to the Supreme Court. Naming the wrong appellate court in the notice is a clerical mistake that is corrected, not a ground for dismissal.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-3-10; Ala. R. App. P. 3(c)"},{"t":"Pour-over transfers","d":"The Supreme Court may transfer to the Court of Civil Appeals any civil appeal within its own jurisdiction, except cases presenting a substantial federal or state constitutional question, a novel legal question with significant statewide impact, utility-rate cases, bond-validation proceedings, and bar discipline.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-2-7(6)"},{"t":"Final judgments and Rule 54(b)","d":"An appeal of right lies from any final judgment of circuit or probate court. A ruling on fewer than all claims or parties is not final unless the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment, or the judgment is final as to served defendants under Civil Rule 4(f). On appeal from a judgment, a party may obtain review of any judgment, order, or ruling of the trial court.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-22-2; Ala. R. Civ. P. 54(b), 4(f); Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals of right (14 days)","d":"Only 14 days to appeal interlocutory orders granting, continuing, modifying, refusing, or dissolving an injunction (or refusing to dissolve or modify one); interlocutory orders appointing or refusing to appoint a receiver; interlocutory orders determining the right to public office; judgments validating public obligations; and final juvenile-court orders.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)(A)–(E)"},{"t":"Arbitration orders are appealable as of right","d":"An order granting or denying a motion to compel arbitration is appealable as of right within 42 days of entry. An order granting or denying a Civil Rule 59 motion to vacate a judgment entered on an arbitration award (Rule 71B) is also appealable within 42 days.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(d), 4(e); Ala. R. Civ. P. 71B"},{"t":"Contempt: pending claim denied by operation of law","d":"A pending contempt claim is denied by operation of law when an order disposes of all other claims, so that order is a final, appealable judgment unless it expressly reserves the contempt claim (effective Mar. 1, 2026). Contempt adjudications are reviewed by appeal.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 70A(g)"},{"t":"Denial of campaign-contribution recusal motion","d":"In civil actions, an order denying a motion to recuse under the campaign-contribution statute is appealable like a final order, but only within 30 days of the denial. If the statutory contribution threshold is met, the trial-court action is stayed in all respects while the appeal is pending.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-24-3(d)"}]],["Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form",[{"t":"42 days, jurisdictional","d":"File the notice of appeal with the trial-court clerk (it may be e-filed through the trial court’s system) within 42 days (6 weeks) of entry of the judgment. Entry is the date the order is input into the State Judicial Information System, or the date a judge electronically transmits it. An untimely appeal must be dismissed, and appellate courts cannot extend the time.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 3(a)(1), 4(a)(1), 2(a)(1), 2(b), 26(b); Ala. R. Civ. P. 58(c)"},{"t":"Tolling motions and the 90-day deemed denial","d":"A post-judgment motion under Civil Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 suspends the appeal time, and the full 42 days run from the order disposing of it. If the trial court does not rule within 90 days, the motion is denied by operation of law and the 42 days run from that date, unless all parties expressly consented on the record to extend the time or the appellate court extended it. A Rule 60(b) motion does not toll.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3); Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1, 60(b)"},{"t":"Premature notices","d":"A notice filed after announcement but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. A notice filed while a Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 motion is pending is held in abeyance and becomes effective when the last such motion is decided.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(4), (5)"},{"t":"Extension only in the trial court, only for lack of notice","d":"The circuit court may extend the appeal time by up to 30 days beyond the original deadline only on a showing of excusable neglect based on a party’s failure to learn of the entry of judgment (likewise for cross-appeals when a party did not learn of another’s notice of appeal). Lack of clerk notice otherwise does not affect the deadline.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d); Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days (2 weeks) after the first notice is filed, or within the original time, whichever expires last.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(2)"},{"t":"Contents, service, docketing statement","d":"The notice must name every appellant and appellee (no “et al.” or “etc.”), designate the judgment or order, and name the court; the designation does not limit review. It must be accompanied by the docketing statement (Form 24, 25, or 26). Appellants serve adverse parties; the clerk serves the appellate clerk, court reporter, and counsel. A missing docketing statement does not void the notice but can lead to a stay, dismissal after notice, or contempt.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 3(c), (d), (e)"},{"t":"Docket fee and security for costs","d":"Civil docket fee is $200, paid when the notice is filed; if the notice is e-filed, the fee goes directly to the appellate clerk and must be received within 7 days. Security for costs must be filed with the notice in the trial court unless exempt or covered by a supersedeas bond.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 35A(a)(1), 12(a), 7"}]],["Stays and supersedeas",[{"t":"Automatic 30-day stay, then bond","d":"No execution issues for 30 days after entry (except injunctions and receiverships). To stay a money judgment on appeal, post a supersedeas bond of 150% of the judgment if it is $10,000 or less, or 125% if more, approved by the trial-court clerk; approval (unless contested) stays the judgment. The State needs no bond. Relief is sought first in the trial court; the appellate court may act if that is impracticable or was denied.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d), (e); Ala. R. App. P. 8(a), (b)"}]],["The record on appeal",[{"t":"Designations and transcript order within 7 days","d":"Within 7 days (1 week) after filing the notice, the appellant must file and serve a designation of the clerk’s record and pay the court reporter’s estimated cost and file the transcript purchase order (Form 1A), with a statement of issues if less than all is designated. If the appellant challenges the sufficiency of the evidence for a finding, all relevant evidence must be in the transcript. The appellee may counter-designate within 7 days after receiving the purchase order.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 10(b)(1), (2)"},{"t":"Completion deadlines","d":"Clerk’s record and certificate of completion: 28 days after the notice of appeal. Reporter’s transcript: 56 days. The clerk assembles the full record within 7 days after the transcript is filed (28 days after the notice if no transcript). The trial court may give up to four 7-day extensions for the transcript, never past 84 days from the notice; further 7-day extensions come only from the appellate court on a timely motion.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 11(a), (c)"},{"t":"No transcript: statement of the evidence","d":"If no report was made or a transcript is unavailable, serve a statement of the evidence within 28 days after the notice; the appellee has 14 days to object or propose amendments; the trial court settles it.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 10(d)"},{"t":"Supplementing or correcting the record","d":"Motions to supplement or correct go first to the trial court; a dissatisfied party may seek appellate relief within 7 days after the order (or after the 14-day period for the trial court to rule).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 10(f)"}]],["Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits",[{"t":"Schedule","d":"Appellant: 28 days after the date on the certificate of completion of the record. Appellee: 21 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply: 14 days after the appellee’s brief. With a cross-appeal: appellee/cross-appellant 21 days; appellant/cross-appellee 21 days; cross-reply 14 days. One extension of up to 7 days may be granted. An appellant who misses the deadline faces a motion to dismiss; an appellee who files no brief is not heard at oral argument without permission.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 31(a), (c), (d)"},{"t":"Word limits and font","d":"Principal brief 14,000 words; reply 7,000 (death cases 16,000/8,000). Counted from the statement of the case onward, including headings, footnotes, and quotations. Motions to exceed must show extraordinary circumstances and be filed at least 7 days before the brief is due; overlength filings without leave may draw a penalty reducing the limit by up to 1,000 words. Century Schoolbook 14, double-spaced, fully justified; certificate of compliance required. Amicus briefs: half the supported party’s limit, by leave only.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 28(j), 32(a)(6)–(7), 32(d), 29"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"Statement on oral argument, table of contents, statement of jurisdiction (with filing dates showing timeliness), table of authorities, statement of the case identifying the adverse rulings and record pages, issues, facts with record cites (“C.” for clerk’s record, “R.” for transcript), standard of review, summary of argument (never more than 5 pages), argument with pinpoint citations (Bluebook, ALWD, or Alabama Supreme Court style), conclusion, certificates of compliance and service. Covers: appellant blue, appellee red, amicus green, reply gray.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 28(a), (d), (g)"},{"t":"Supplemental authority","d":"Notice of supplemental authority by letter, 400 words maximum, stating the reasons and the brief page it supports; responses similarly limited.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 28B"}]],["Motions, rehearing, and certiorari",[{"t":"Motions","d":"Responses within 7 days; replies within 7 days after the response. Procedural motions (including extensions) may be decided without waiting for a response. Limits: motion 2,000 words, supporting or opposing memorandum 3,000, reply 1,000. Emergency motions need the capitalized caption, a ruling-by date, and a statement that the movant contacted the clerk’s office at least a day ahead and consulted opposing counsel.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 27"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, brief required","d":"A party that did not prevail may apply for rehearing within 14 days of the decision, filing a supporting brief with the application (3,000 words each). Only a party that filed a brief on original submission may apply as of right. A second application is considered only if the first produced a reversal or substantial modification. Opposition brief, if any, is due within 14 days.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 40(a)–(c), (f), (g)"},{"t":"Certiorari to the Supreme Court from the Court of Civil Appeals","d":"Discretionary, and granted only on listed grounds: a decision first holding a statute or ordinance valid or invalid or first construing a controlling constitutional provision; one affecting a class of constitutional, state, or county officers; a question of first impression for the Supreme Court; a conflict with prior decisions of the U.S. Supreme Court, the Supreme Court of Alabama, or either Alabama court of appeals (the conflicting passages must be quoted); or a request to overrule controlling Supreme Court precedent. File within 14 days of the Court of Civil Appeals decision, or of its ruling on rehearing if one was sought. Rehearing is not a prerequisite in civil cases. Petition limit 3,000 words; no briefs before the writ issues unless ordered. The Supreme Court cannot extend the time (except in death cases).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 39(a)(1), (c), (d); 2(b); 26(b)"},{"t":"Facts on certiorari come from the rehearing application","d":"If you disagree with the Court of Civil Appeals’ statement of facts (or it issued a no-opinion affirmance), put your proposed additional, corrected, or own statement of facts in the application for rehearing; the certiorari petition must copy that statement verbatim with record references, with a verification when you propose your own statement.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 39(d)(5), 40(e)"},{"t":"Certificate of judgment","d":"Issues 18 days after entry of judgment (or 18 days after denial of rehearing in the courts of appeals). In the Supreme Court, denial of rehearing issues the certificate immediately unless the first application produced a substantial modification.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 41(a)"},{"t":"No-opinion affirmances","d":"The appellate courts may affirm without opinion in specified circumstances.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 53, 54"}]],["Interlocutory review by permission and by mandamus",[{"t":"Permissive appeal (Rule 5)","d":"Available only in civil cases within the Supreme Court’s original appellate jurisdiction (not Court of Civil Appeals cases). The trial judge must certify a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, that an immediate appeal would materially advance termination, and that it would avoid protracted and expensive litigation, stating the question. Certification is presumptively timely within 28 days of the order; the petition is due in the Supreme Court within 14 days after certification (4,000 words); answers within 14 days; no reply. If granted, file security for costs and the docket fee within 14 days. No automatic stay.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)–(e)"},{"t":"Mandamus and prohibition","d":"A petition for a writ directed to a trial judge goes to the appellate court with jurisdiction, with a full appendix of the order, related filings, and transcripts (6,000 words). It must be filed within a reasonable time, presumptively the same as the time for appeal (42 days); a later petition must show good cause. Review in the Supreme Court of a court of appeals’ decision on an original writ petition is by a new petition filed within 14 days.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 21(a)(1), (3), (d), (e)"}]],["Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing",[{"t":"Mailbox and carrier rules","d":"A paper filing is timely only if received by the deadline, except it is deemed filed on the mailing date if sent by USPS certified, registered, or express mail, or on the date a commercial carrier receives it for delivery within 3 calendar days (with the carrier’s dated documentation).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 25(a)(3)"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the trigger day; roll a last day falling on a weekend, holiday, or inaccessible-clerk day to the next business day; exclude intermediate weekends and holidays only for periods under 7 days. Holidays include those set by the governor, chief justice, legislature, President, or Congress.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 26(a)"},{"t":"Appellate e-filing (ACIS)","d":"Attorneys may e-file in the appellate courts after registering; pro se parties are excluded from e-filing (except records). Licensed Alabama lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers admitted in the appeal may register. A document is filed when successfully uploaded, measured in Central time; PDFs at 200 DPI or more, with bookmarks for each section. In the Supreme Court, e-filed briefs and petitions still require 2 additional hard copies. A filing missed because of a technology problem on the due date needs a motion to accept it as timely.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 57; 31(b); 21(d); 5(e)"},{"t":"Signatures and certifications","d":"Every brief or motion must be signed by counsel of record with address and telephone; signing certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and that factual statements are supported in the record.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 25A"}]],["District-court judgments",[{"t":"Appeal to circuit court for trial de novo","d":"Final district-court judgments are appealed to circuit court for a new trial, not to an appellate court (with exceptions in § 12-12-72 and juvenile cases). An appellant who wants a jury must demand it in the notice of appeal; the appellee must demand within 14 days of service of the notice. The time to take the de novo appeal is set by statute and was not collected. Compulsory counterclaims must be added within 30 days after the appeal is perfected.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-12-71; Ala. R. App. P. 4(b)(2); Ala. R. Civ. P. 13(j), 38(b)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from final civil judgment","d":"42 days from entry of judgment (input into SJIS or e-transmission by judge).\nJurisdictional; no appellate extension (Rules 2(b), 26(b)).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1); Ala. R. Civ. P. 58(c)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional; no appellate extension (Rules 2(b), 26(b))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after post-judgment motion","d":"42 days from entry of order disposing of last Rule 50/52/55/59 motion, or deemed-denial date.\nRule 60(b) motions do not toll.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3); Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of order disposing of last Rule 50/52/55/59 motion, or deemed-denial date)","note":"Rule 60(b) motions do not toll."}},{"t":"Post-judgment motion deemed denied","d":"90 days from filing of the Rule 50/52/55/59 motion.\nUnless all parties consent on the record or the appellate court extends. District court: 14 days.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Rule 50/52/55/59 motion)","note":"Unless all parties consent on the record or the appellate court extends. District court: 14 days."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial / to alter, amend, or vacate / renewed JML / amend findings","d":"30 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Rule 6(b)). District court 14 days; eviction and unlawful detainer 7 calendar days.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)). District court 14 days; eviction and unlawful detainer 7 calendar days."}},{"t":"Motion to set aside default judgment","d":"30 days from entry of default judgment.\nTolls under App. Rule 4(a)(3); 59.1 applies.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 55(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of default judgment)","note":"Tolls under App. Rule 4(a)(3); 59.1 applies."}},{"t":"Appeal of interlocutory injunction, receivership, public-office, bond-validation, or juvenile order","d":"14 days from entry of the order.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)(A)–(E)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":""}},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"14 days from filing of first notice of appeal (or original 42 days, whichever later).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Appeal of order granting or denying motion to compel arbitration","d":"42 days from entry of the order.\nAppeal as of right.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 4(d)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Appeal as of right."}},{"t":"Trial-court extension of appeal time for failure to learn of entry","d":"30 days from expiration of original appeal time.\nExcusable neglect based on lack of notice only.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of original appeal time)","note":"Excusable neglect based on lack of notice only."}},{"t":"Appeal of denial of campaign-contribution recusal motion","d":"30 days from order denying recusal.\nCivil actions; trial stayed if statutory threshold met.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-24-3(d)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (order denying recusal)","note":"Civil actions; trial stayed if statutory threshold met."}},{"t":"Designation of clerk’s record; transcript purchase order and payment","d":"7 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nAppellee counter-designation 7 days after receipt.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 10(b)(1), (2)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"Appellee counter-designation 7 days after receipt."}},{"t":"Docket fee received by appellate clerk (e-filed notice)","d":"7 days from e-filing of notice of appeal.\n$200 civil.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 12(a), 35A","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (e-filing of notice of appeal)","note":"$200 civil."}},{"t":"Clerk’s record completed","d":"28 days from filing of notice of appeal.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(1)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Reporter’s transcript filed","d":"56 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nTrial-court extensions cannot go beyond 84 days from the notice.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(2), 11(c)","due":{"n":56,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"Trial-court extensions cannot go beyond 84 days from the notice."}},{"t":"Statement of the evidence served (no transcript)","d":"28 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nObjections 14 days after service.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 10(d)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"Objections 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"28 days from date on certificate of completion of the record.\nOne extension up to 7 days (Rule 31(d)).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (date on certificate of completion of the record)","note":"One extension up to 7 days (Rule 31(d))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"21 days from filing of appellant’s brief.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from filing of appellee’s brief.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellee’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellant/cross-appellee brief in cross-appeal","d":"21 days from filing of appellee/cross-appellant’s brief.\nCross-reply 14 days after.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellee/cross-appellant’s brief)","note":"Cross-reply 14 days after."}},{"t":"Motion to exceed word limit","d":"7 days from before the brief’s due date.\nFiled at least 7 days before due date.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 28(j)(3)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Filed at least 7 days before due date."}},{"t":"Response to motion","d":"7 days from service of motion.\nReply 7 days after response.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 27(a)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (service of motion)","note":"Reply 7 days after response."}},{"t":"Application for rehearing (with brief)","d":"14 days from release of decision.\nBrief on original submission required.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 40(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (release of decision)","note":"Brief on original submission required."}},{"t":"Petition for certiorari to Supreme Court from Court of Civil Appeals","d":"14 days from court of Civil Appeals decision, or its ruling on rehearing.\nCannot be extended in civil cases (Rule 2(b)).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 39(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (court of Civil Appeals decision, or its ruling on rehearing)","note":"Cannot be extended in civil cases (Rule 2(b))."}},{"t":"Certificate of judgment issues","d":"18 days from entry of appellate judgment (or denial of rehearing in courts of appeals).","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 41(a)","due":{"n":18,"from":"the trigger date (entry of appellate judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Trial judge’s Rule 5 certification (presumptive)","d":"28 days from entry of interlocutory order.\nLater certification requires good-cause statement.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of interlocutory order)","note":"Later certification requires good-cause statement."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal (Rule 5)","d":"14 days from entry of trial judge’s certification.\nSupreme Court cases only; answer 14 days.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of trial judge’s certification)","note":"Supreme Court cases only; answer 14 days."}},{"t":"Mandamus petition (presumptively reasonable time)","d":"42 days from entry of challenged order.\nLater petitions must show good cause.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 21(a)(3)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of challenged order)","note":"Later petitions must show good cause."}},{"t":"Supreme Court review of court of appeals ruling on original writ petition","d":"14 days from grant or denial by court of appeals.","c":"Ala. R. App. P. 21(e)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (grant or denial by court of appeals)","note":""}},{"t":"Automatic stay of execution","d":"30 days from entry of judgment.\nNot for injunction or receivership judgments; district court 14 days.","c":"Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Not for injunction or receivership judgments; district court 14 days."}},{"t":"Jury demand by appellee in de novo appeal to circuit court","d":"14 days from service of notice of appeal.\nAppellant must demand in the notice of appeal.","c":"Ala. Code § 12-12-71","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of notice of appeal)","note":"Appellant must demand in the notice of appeal."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal from final civil judgment",42,"Entry of judgment (input into SJIS or e-transmission by judge)","Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1); Ala. R. Civ. P. 58(c)","Jurisdictional; no appellate extension (Rules 2(b), 26(b))."],["Notice of appeal after post-judgment motion",42,"Entry of order disposing of last Rule 50/52/55/59 motion, or deemed-denial date","Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3); Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","Rule 60(b) motions do not toll."],["Post-judgment motion deemed denied",90,"Filing of the Rule 50/52/55/59 motion","Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","Unless all parties consent on the record or the appellate court extends. District court: 14 days."],["Motion for new trial / to alter, amend, or vacate / renewed JML / amend findings",30,"Entry of judgment","Ala. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","Cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)). District court 14 days; eviction and unlawful detainer 7 calendar days."],["Motion to set aside default judgment",30,"Entry of default judgment","Ala. R. Civ. P. 55(c)","Tolls under App. Rule 4(a)(3); 59.1 applies."],["Appeal of interlocutory injunction, receivership, public-office, bond-validation, or juvenile order",14,"Entry of the order","Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)(A)–(E)",""],["Cross-appeal",14,"Filing of first notice of appeal (or original 42 days, whichever later)","Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(2)",""],["Appeal of order granting or denying motion to compel arbitration",42,"Entry of the order","Ala. R. App. P. 4(d)","Appeal as of right."],["Trial-court extension of appeal time for failure to learn of entry",30,"Expiration of original appeal time","Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","Excusable neglect based on lack of notice only."],["Appeal of denial of campaign-contribution recusal motion",30,"Order denying recusal","Ala. Code § 12-24-3(d)","Civil actions; trial stayed if statutory threshold met."],["Designation of clerk’s record; transcript purchase order and payment",7,"Filing of notice of appeal","Ala. R. App. P. 10(b)(1), (2)","Appellee counter-designation 7 days after receipt."],["Docket fee received by appellate clerk (e-filed notice)",7,"E-filing of notice of appeal","Ala. R. App. P. 12(a), 35A","$200 civil."],["Clerk’s record completed",28,"Filing of notice of appeal","Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(1)",""],["Reporter’s transcript filed",56,"Filing of notice of appeal","Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(2), 11(c)","Trial-court extensions cannot go beyond 84 days from the notice."],["Statement of the evidence served (no transcript)",28,"Filing of notice of appeal","Ala. R. App. P. 10(d)","Objections 14 days after service."],["Appellant’s brief",28,"Date on certificate of completion of the record","Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","One extension up to 7 days (Rule 31(d))."],["Appellee’s brief",21,"Filing of appellant’s brief","Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)",""],["Reply brief",14,"Filing of appellee’s brief","Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)",""],["Appellant/cross-appellee brief in cross-appeal",21,"Filing of appellee/cross-appellant’s brief","Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","Cross-reply 14 days after."],["Motion to exceed word limit",7,"Before the brief’s due date","Ala. R. App. P. 28(j)(3)","Filed at least 7 days before due date."],["Response to motion",7,"Service of motion","Ala. R. App. P. 27(a)","Reply 7 days after response."],["Application for rehearing (with brief)",14,"Release of decision","Ala. R. App. P. 40(c)","Brief on original submission required."],["Petition for certiorari to Supreme Court from Court of Civil Appeals",14,"Court of Civil Appeals decision, or its ruling on rehearing","Ala. R. App. P. 39(c)","Cannot be extended in civil cases (Rule 2(b))."],["Certificate of judgment issues",18,"Entry of appellate judgment (or denial of rehearing in courts of appeals)","Ala. R. App. P. 41(a)",""],["Trial judge’s Rule 5 certification (presumptive)",28,"Entry of interlocutory order","Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","Later certification requires good-cause statement."],["Petition for permission to appeal (Rule 5)",14,"Entry of trial judge’s certification","Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(2)","Supreme Court cases only; answer 14 days."],["Mandamus petition (presumptively reasonable time)",42,"Entry of challenged order","Ala. R. App. P. 21(a)(3)","Later petitions must show good cause."],["Supreme Court review of court of appeals ruling on original writ petition",14,"Grant or denial by court of appeals","Ala. R. App. P. 21(e)(2)",""],["Automatic stay of execution",30,"Entry of judgment","Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a)","Not for injunction or receivership judgments; district court 14 days."],["Jury demand by appellee in de novo appeal to circuit court",14,"Service of notice of appeal","Ala. Code § 12-12-71","Appellant must demand in the notice of appeal."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://judicial.alabama.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"Govern civil actions in the circuit courts, in the district courts as modified by each rule’s “(dc)” subdivision, in small-claims courts through Small Claims Rule N, in probate courts where appropriate, and in other courts whose appeals go directly to the Supreme Court or Court of Civil Appeals. Construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.” In district court it is subject to the statutory limits on equitable jurisdiction (§ 12-12-30).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint. Except in domestic-relations cases, a circuit-court complaint (and a notice of appeal to circuit court from district or probate court) must be filed with a signed informational cover sheet (Form 93); a missing sheet does not void the filing, but the court may stay or, after notice, dismiss until one is filed (amended eff. Sept. 3, 2025).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"4","t":"Process: general provisions","s":"The clerk issues the summons on filing. Service must be made within 120 days of filing or the court, after at least 14 days’ notice, may dismiss without prejudice (time must be extended for good cause). Service may be made by sheriff or constable, by any nonparty at least 19 years old not related within the third degree, by certified mail (requested of the clerk or initiated by the attorney with an “Affidavit of Certified Mailing”), or by approved commercial carrier (Rule 4(i)(3), added Aug. 14, 2023, with a proof of service due within 10 days of receiving the carrier’s receipt). Certified-mail service is complete on delivery to the addressee or the addressee’s authorized agent, and refused service is completed by the clerk’s ordinary mailing (Rule 4(e)). Unlike Federal Rule 4, there is no waiver-of-service-by-mail procedure with cost shifting; a defendant may simply accept or waive service in a signed filed document (Rule 4(h)).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Service of other process","s":"Process other than a summons or subpoena is served by a sheriff, constable, or specially appointed person, anywhere in Alabama and outside it when authorized by law.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Basis for and methods of out-of-state service","s":"Out-of-state service is proper whenever the defendant has contacts with Alabama such that suit here is consistent with the Alabama and U.S. Constitutions (Alabama’s long-arm reaches the due-process limit). Also reaches personal representatives and guardians sued for a decedent’s or ward’s acts.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"4.3","t":"Service by publication","s":"Publication is available for certain historically equitable claims involving property or marital status where identity or residence is unknown or a resident has been absent more than 30 days, and for any claim against a defendant who avoids service. A filed affidavit must state specific facts of avoidance (mere failure of service is not enough). Notice runs once a week for 4 successive weeks; the answer is due 30 days after the last publication; service is complete on the last publication. In domestic cases, first-class mail may replace publication on a hardship affidavit, with service complete on the third day after mailing. No in personam judgment on publication except for avoidance of service.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"4.4","t":"Service in a foreign country","s":"Allows service abroad by internationally agreed means (e.g., the Hague Service Convention), by foreign-law methods, by letter rogatory, by personal delivery or clerk-dispatched signed-receipt mail unless foreign law forbids it, or by other court-directed means not barred by treaty. Tracks Federal Rule 4(f).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint go to every party, through counsel when represented. Service is by hand, leaving at office or residence, mail (complete on mailing), filing with the e-filing system to a registered user (complete on sending), or, as of Oct. 1, 2026, e-mail to an unrepresented party at the e-mail address that party listed. Every filed paper must state the filer’s address, e-mail address, and telephone number. Discovery papers (depositions, notices, interrogatories, document requests, requests for admission, and responses) are not filed unless used at trial, in a motion, or ordered, but may be served through the e-filing system without filing.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Privacy protection for court filings","s":"Filings may show only the last four digits of Social Security, taxpayer-ID, and financial-account numbers, with listed exemptions. The filer, not the clerk, is responsible for redaction. Filing one’s own information unredacted and not under seal waives protection.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"6","t":"Computing and extending time; time for motion papers","s":"Exclude the trigger day; if the period is less than 11 days, also exclude intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays (the federal rule dropped this in 2009). The last day rolls forward if it is a weekend, holiday, or (for filings) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible. Legal holidays include those set by Ala. Code § 1-3-8 and Juneteenth; Rule 6 was amended effective Apr. 9, 2026. Extensions require good cause, but the court cannot extend time under Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b). Written motions and notices of hearing must be served at least 5 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits at least 1 day before. Add 3 days after service by mail or through the e-filing system. In district court, the weekend-exclusion rule does not apply in unlawful-detainer and eviction cases.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; a reply to an answer only if ordered. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state grounds with particularity, and be signed under Rule 11. Demurrers and pleas are abolished.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Notice pleading: a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief. Affirmative defenses must be pleaded, and the Alabama list includes contributory negligence, assumption of risk, and injury by fellow servant. Averments not denied are admitted, except as to the amount of damage.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; capacity is challenged by specific negative averment. Rule 9(h) allows fictitious-party pleading: a plaintiff ignorant of a defendant’s name may sue that party by a fictitious name and substitute the true name later (the federal rules have no equivalent). Relation back of the substitution is addressed in Rule 15(c)(4).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption with court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; separate counts for separate transactions where helpful. Exhibits attached to a pleading are part of it.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"11","t":"Signature, required information, certification, sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) and, as of Oct. 1, 2026, state the signer’s address, e-mail address, and telephone number. The signature certifies only that the signer read the paper, that there is good ground to support it, and that it is not interposed for delay. A ghostwriting lawyer helping a self-represented person need not sign but must add the certification that the document was prepared with the assistance of a licensed Alabama lawyer under Rule 1.2(c), Ala. R. Prof. Cond. The only sanctions in the rule are striking an unsigned or bad-faith paper and discipline for willful violation; there is no federal-style 11(b) list, 21-day safe harbor, or monetary sanction scheme in the rule text.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"The answer is due 30 days after service (not 21 as in federal court); a reply to a counterclaim is due 30 days after service of the answer. A Rule 12 motion moves the deadline to 10 days after notice of the ruling (or after service of a more definite statement). The seven Rule 12(b) defenses and the waiver rules track federal practice; a motion to strike is due within 30 days if no response is permitted. District court: 14 days to answer, 7 calendar days in unlawful-detainer and eviction cases, and no optional Rule 12(b) motions.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Compulsory counterclaims must be pleaded, with three exceptions: the claim was already pending elsewhere, the opposing suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the opposing claim is for damage covered by a liability policy under which the insurer controls the defense; the rule warns res judicata may still apply. In a de novo appeal to circuit court, a compulsory counterclaim must be added within 30 days after the appeal is perfected, and Rule 13(j) sets recovery caps tied to the lower court’s jurisdiction. In district court a counterclaim beyond its jurisdiction is not compulsory.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave within 10 days after serving its original answer (not 14 as in federal court); later requires leave by motion. As amended effective Oct. 16, 2026, the rule follows the restyled federal structure, including the third-party defendant’s defenses and claims. A plaintiff who fails to assert a claim against a third-party defendant arising from the same transaction suffers the same effect as failing to assert a compulsory counterclaim (Rule 14(a)(3)), a trap with no federal counterpart. Does not apply on the district court small-claims docket.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend without leave at any time more than 42 days before the first trial setting (unless the court orders otherwise), subject to the court’s disallowance or a motion to strike; after that, leave requires good cause. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left to respond to the original or 10 days after service, whichever is longer (7 days in district court). Relation back covers changed parties if notice and mistake are shown within the limitations period or 120 days after commencement, whichever is later, and separately allows relation back under Rule 9(h) fictitious-party practice.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The court may hold pretrial conferences and enter a scheduling order, which may be modified only for good cause. Without an order, any party may require a conference at least 21 days before trial. A final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sanctions include Rule 37(b)(2)(B)–(D) orders and mandatory expenses unless substantially justified. Initial disclosures and a Rule 26(f) meeting are not required.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties; capacity","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed to ratify or substitute. A guardian ad litem must be appointed for a minor defendant, and a minor 14 or older may nominate a guardian ad litem within 30 days of service (14 in district court). The rule bars appointment of a guardian ad litem related to or recommended by the plaintiff or plaintiff’s counsel.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"Any number of legal and equitable claims may be joined. A creditor may join a money claim with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance. Rule 18(c): no rule permits a jury to try an insurance-coverage question together with the related damage claim against the insured.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Tracks the pre-2007 federal rule: join required persons if feasible; if not, decide in equity and good conscience whether to proceed using four factors. A joined party who objects to venue and whose joinder makes venue improper must be dismissed.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question; the court may order separate trials.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"As amended effective Oct. 16, 2026: a plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may interplead claimants even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability. The stakeholder may deposit the money or property with the court and be discharged. A party that requested attorney fees in a pleading may be awarded a reasonable fee from the fund (no federal-rule counterpart).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Rule 23(a)–(b) track the federal prerequisites and categories. Certification is decided at an early practicable time “consistent with Alabama statutory law” (the statute is not named in the rule and was not collected). Notice for (b)(3) classes may be by mail, electronic, or other means. Settlement, dismissal, or compromise of a certified or proposed settlement class needs court approval and notice. Not available in district court.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"A verified complaint must allege contemporaneous ownership and particularized demand efforts or why none was made; court approval is needed to dismiss or compromise.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Unincorporated associations","s":"Actions by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class require representatives who fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention as of right and permissive intervention on timely motion, with a pleading setting out the claim or defense. Tracks the pre-restyling federal rule.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After death is suggested on the record by service, a motion to substitute must be made within 6 months or the action is dismissed as to the decedent absent excusable neglect (federal: 90 days). Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Scope: any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action (broader than the federal claim-or-defense standard), proportional to the needs of the case, and the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language is retained. No initial disclosures and no automatic expert reports; expert information comes through interrogatories. A privilege log is due within 21 days after a written request. Motions for protective orders require a good-faith conferral statement. District court: discovery only by court leave or agreement, and depositions only if the witness will be unavailable for trial.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"27","t":"Discovery before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition in the circuit court of an expected adverse party’s county may seek depositions or Rule 34/35 discovery before suit, with notice served at least 30 days before the hearing. Similar relief is available pending appeal. Not available in district court.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Officers authorized by U.S., Alabama, or local law, or court appointees; abroad, by notice, commission, letters rogatory, or treaty. Rule 28(c) lets parties in other states’ cases obtain Alabama subpoenas from a circuit judge where the witness resides on a commission or proof of notice. No relatives, employees, or financially interested persons may serve as officer.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify other discovery procedures.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given. No 7-hour or 10-deposition limits appear in the rule. A party deponent may be asked to bring documents; objections are due within 14 days (45 days if served with the complaint). Remote depositions and remote oaths by stipulation or order; objections must be concise and non-argumentative; instructions not to answer only for privilege, court limits, or a Rule 30(d)(2) motion (amended eff. Mar. 14, 2024). The witness has 30 days to read and sign. The court may sanction anyone who impedes or frustrates a fair examination.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Cross questions within 15 days of the notice, redirect within 10 days, recross within 5 days. Any party may demand to attend and cross-examine orally within the cross-question period.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, or unavailable by subpoena, and (unlike the federal rule) whenever the witness is a licensed physician or dentist. Waiver rules for errors and irregularities track the federal rule; objections to the form of written questions are due within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Limit of 40 interrogatories per party, counting each subpart separately, with “party” including all parties represented by the same lawyer or firm (federal: 25). Answers are due in 30 days, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. A motion to compel must quote the interrogatory and objection in full.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things","s":"Response due in 30 days (defendant: 45 days after service of process). Requests may specify the ESI form; documents must be produced as kept or organized by request category. Nonparties are reached under Rule 45.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause, when a condition is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. Report exchange and privilege waiver track the older federal rule. Not available in district court.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days (defendants need not respond earlier than 45 days after service of process). Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion. No numerical limit appears in the rule.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"A motion to compel must include a good-faith conferral statement. Expenses are mandatory to the winner unless substantially justified. Sanctions for violating orders range from deemed facts to dismissal or default. Rule 37(g) (eff. Dec. 21, 2018) adopts the federal Rule 37(e) framework for lost ESI: curative measures on prejudice; adverse inference, dismissal, or default only on intent to deprive.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"A jury demand must be served within 30 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14). In an appeal or certiorari to circuit court, the appellant must demand within 10 days after filing and others within 10 days after service. Failure to demand, or to appear at trial, waives the jury. A demand is deemed a demand for a struck jury. Not applicable in district court (no juries there).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no jury right exists; the court may order a jury despite a missing demand and may use an advisory jury.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"Trials must be set at least 60 days in advance (30 days for de novo appeals; 14 days in district court), with listed exceptions. The clerk must notify out-of-county attorneys within 3 days after a case is placed on the trial docket.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"Voluntary dismissal by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissals are with prejudice unless the order says otherwise (except jurisdiction, venue, and Rule 19). Costs of a prior dismissed action may be ordered.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Consolidation for common questions; separate trials for convenience, prejudice, or economy, preserving the jury right under Ala. Const. art. I, § 11.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken orally in open court; remote testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances. Uncontested divorce testimony is taken before the clerk unless the judge orders otherwise. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits. Admissibility is governed by statute and the Alabama Rules of Evidence.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of documents","s":"Authentication of domestic and foreign official records, plus Alabama-specific provisions on altered documents, recorded instruments (prima facie evidence), judgments, learned treatises and maps, business records, private documents with subscribing witnesses, and handwriting comparison.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party raising another state’s or country’s law must give written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and the determination is a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Trial and deposition subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending. A document-only subpoena requires 15 days’ notice to all parties with the proposed subpoena attached (not before 45 days after service on a defendant unless that defendant has sought discovery); any objection within 10 days blocks issuance until the court rules. The subpoena must allow at least 15 days to comply. Service by sheriff, nonparty aged 19 or older, certified mail, or commercial carrier (amended eff. Aug. 14, 2023). A nonparty may not be compelled to travel more than 100 miles, except a person within the state may be compelled to attend trial anywhere in the state.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are abolished; a party preserves error by making known the action desired or the objection and its grounds when the ruling is made.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Voir dire by court or counsel. Civil juries are “struck”: parties alternately strike from a list of at least 24 qualified jurors until 12 remain, the demanding party striking first. Up to 6 alternates, chosen from the last names struck; alternates may be retained during deliberations, with instructions to start anew if one is substituted. With multiple parties, the plaintiff side gets one-half of the total strikes.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than twelve; majority verdict","s":"Parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors or to a majority verdict; otherwise the default is 12 jurors.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"49","t":"General verdicts, special verdicts, and interrogatories","s":"General verdicts are the norm; special verdicts or interrogatories may be used, but only if counsel receive the questions a reasonable time before closing arguments.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law","s":"JML motions may be made before submission to the jury. A renewed motion is due within 30 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), with an alternative new-trial motion. A party against whom JML is entered has 30 days to move for a new trial.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury; objection","s":"Written requested charges are marked “given” or “refused.” The court instructs after closing arguments, and given charges are read without identifying the requesting party. To preserve error, a party must object before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds. The judge may not comment on the evidence.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","s":"Findings of fact are made on written request or when required by statute (not automatically, as in federal court). A motion to amend findings is due within 30 days (14 days in district court; 7 calendar days in unlawful-detainer and eviction cases). Sufficiency of the evidence may be raised on appeal without a post-trial motion when findings were made.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Reference is the exception. In nonjury cases, findings stand unless clearly erroneous; objections within 10 days after notice of the report’s filing. Masters may not serve when related to a party, stockholder, or attorney within the fourth degree.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Rule 54(b) certification requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry of judgment; otherwise a partial ruling is not final. A judgment may also be final as to served defendants under Rule 4(f). Costs go to the prevailing party; review of the clerk’s taxation must be sought within 5 days after notice. Attorney-fee motion procedure under Federal Rule 54(d)(2) has no counterpart in the text.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default; the clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain against a nonappearing adult defendant. If the defaulting party has appeared, a hearing is required with at least 3 days’ written notice (not required on the trial date). The court may set aside a default before judgment, and a default judgment on its own motion or on a party’s motion filed within 30 days after entry (14 in district court; 7 in unlawful-detainer and eviction). No default judgment in divorce or against minors or incompetents without proof. Rule 55(b)(2) was amended effective May 1, 2023.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move 30 days after commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must include a narrative summary of undisputed material facts with record citations, and the opponent must file a statement in opposition supported the same way. The motion and all supporting material must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, and opposition at least 2 days before (district court: 14 and 7 days). The rule text still uses the pre-2010 federal structure (Rule 56(f) affidavits for more discovery; bad-faith affidavit sanctions).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Procedure under Ala. Code §§ 6-6-220 to 6-6-232 follows these rules; a jury may be demanded; another adequate remedy does not bar relief. Not available in district court.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"58","t":"Rendition and entry of orders and judgments","s":"A judge renders an order by separate document, opinion, notation (“granted,” “denied”) on a motion, notation in the record, or electronic transmission to the e-filing system. An order is “entered” when input into the State Judicial Information System, or when the judge electronically transmits it, and that date starts appeal and post-judgment clocks. No separate-document requirement. Interest runs from rendition.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"A new-trial motion and a motion to alter, amend, or vacate must be filed within 30 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28); the court may order a new trial on its own within 30 days. Opposing affidavits are due 15 days after service, extendable up to 20 more days. Remittitur acceptance does not prejudice cross-appeal. Post-trial motions stay pending until ruled on, subject to Rule 59.1. District court: 14 days (7 calendar days in unlawful-detainer and eviction). Amended eff. Apr. 9, 2026 (Rule 59(a)).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"59.1","t":"Disposition of post-judgment motions (deemed denial)","s":"A post-judgment motion under Rules 50, 52, 55, or 59 is automatically denied by operation of law if not ruled on within 90 days, unless all parties expressly consent on the record or the appellate court extends the time. The 42-day appeal clock then runs from the deemed-denial date, whether or not the court later acts. District court: 14 days, extendable once by up to 14 days. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical errors may be corrected at any time. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule; motions on grounds (1)–(3) must be filed within 4 months (federal: 1 year). An independent action may be brought within 3 years (or longer under §§ 6-2-3 and 6-2-8). A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality or toll the time to appeal; leave of the appellate court is needed only while an appeal is actually pending.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Automatic stay of execution for 30 days after entry (14 in district court; 7 in eviction cases). Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A stay on appeal is obtained by a supersedeas bond approved by the court; no bond is required of the State. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions. In district court, execution is automatically stayed pending post-trial motions, and a costs bond or substantial-hardship affidavit replaces the supersedeas bond.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"A successor judge may continue a trial or hearing after certifying familiarity with the record; in nonjury trials, must recall disputed material witnesses on request.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"Pre-judgment seizure (attachment, detinue) requires a sworn affidavit and judicial examination. If the court finds a real risk to the property, the writ may issue without a hearing, but the defendant may demand a hearing within 5 days of seizure, and the writ then expires on the 15th day unless continued after a hearing.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"64A","t":"Notice of right to claim exemption from garnishment","s":"When garnishment issues on a judgment where the defendant did not appear, notice of exemption rights in a prescribed form must be served at least 5 days before judgment against the garnishee. The form notes up to $7,500 in personal property may be exempt under state law.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"64B","t":"Contest of claim of exemption (garnishment)","s":"The defendant files a sworn claim of exemption. If the plaintiff does not contest within 15 calendar days after the claim is filed, the garnishment is dismissed or modified. A contest hearing must be set within 7 calendar days after the contest is filed.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction without notice. A TRO without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint and a lawyer’s written certificate of notice efforts, and expires within 10 days unless extended (the 10-day limit does not apply in domestic-relations cases). The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is mandatory, except for the State and, at the court’s discretion, in domestic cases. Not available in district court.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security; proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as its agent; liability is enforced by motion.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action with a receiver cannot be dismissed without court order; administration follows Ala. Code §§ 6-6-620 to 6-6-628.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"With leave and notice, a party may deposit money or property in court; money goes into an interest-bearing insured account and is paid out only on court order.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A defending party may serve an offer more than 15 days before trial (federal: 14); the offeree has 10 days to accept. If the final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Only defending parties may make offers. District court: 14 and 7 days.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"The clerk issues the writ on application, with a notice of exemption rights (Form 92) served by the sheriff at levy. The debtor may file a notarized claim of exemption before sale; the creditor must contest within the time in § 6-10-26. Post-judgment discovery is allowed under these rules.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title directly by judgment.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"70A","t":"Contempt in civil cases","s":"Defines direct, constructive, criminal, and civil contempt. Direct contempt may be punished summarily with a written order; sentence within 7 days if not imposed immediately. Constructive contempt starts by petition (or counterclaim) with notice and is governed by the civil rules; indigent alleged criminal contemnors get appointed counsel on request. Contempt judgments are reviewed by appeal. New Rule 70A(g)(3) (eff. Mar. 1, 2026): a pending contempt claim is denied by operation of law when an order disposes of all other claims, so that order is final and appealable unless it expressly reserves the contempt claim.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for or against nonparties","s":"A nonparty may enforce an order in its favor, and be bound by enforcement of an order against it, as if a party.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"71A","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"Omitted; Alabama eminent-domain procedure is governed by statute (Title 18).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"71B","t":"Appeals from arbitration awards","s":"A party must file a notice of appeal of an arbitration award in circuit court within 30 days after service of notice of the award, or review is waived. The clerk or court enters the award as a judgment; a party opposing it must then file a Rule 59 motion to vacate on grounds in Ala. Code § 6-6-14 or other law, and the ruling on that motion is appealed under Rule 4, Ala. R. App. P. Subdivision (f) amended eff. Oct. 16, 2026.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"71C","t":"Enforcement of arbitration awards","s":"If no Rule 71B appeal is filed within 30 days after service of notice of the award, a party may move in circuit court for entry of judgment on the award; the clerk or court enters it promptly, and execution follows. Amended eff. Oct. 16, 2026.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"72-76","t":"Appeals","s":"Omitted; appellate procedure is governed by the Alabama Rules of Appellate Procedure.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"77","t":"Circuit courts and clerks","s":"Courts are always open for filing. The clerk serves notice of entry of orders by mail or electronically. Lack of clerk notice does not extend appeal time, except the circuit court may extend the time to appeal (or cross-appeal) by up to 30 days for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry (or of another party’s notice of appeal).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion day","s":"Courts may decide motions on written submissions and may deny a motion to dismiss without a hearing unless a hearing is requested. Unless the court orders otherwise, an order granting a motion to dismiss carries an automatic right to amend within 10 days after service of the order (no federal counterpart).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk","s":"Civil docket, minute book of final judgments, indices, and calendars; records may be kept electronically; files may not be removed without permission.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic transcript as evidence","s":"Prior testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a certified transcript.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability in general","s":"Lists statutory proceedings (e.g., mandamus and other remedial writs, election contests, workers’ compensation, will contests, quo warranto) where these rules apply to the extent the statute does not provide the practice. Not applicable to arbitrators or agencies. Translates older terms (demurrer, bill in equity, decree pro confesso) to their rule equivalents.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue","s":"Rules do not change jurisdiction or venue statutes but resolve conflicts for individual defendants: residents are sued in the county of residence, or for personal non-contract actions in the county where the act or omission occurred; real-estate actions where the land lies. When claims or parties are joined, venue proper for one claim is proper for all. On timely motion the court must transfer (not dismiss) an action filed in the wrong county. If the defendant who made venue proper is later dismissed, remaining defendants may move to transfer within 30 days of notice (all defendants agreeing makes transfer mandatory). Fictitious defendants still unnamed 75 days after a transfer motion do not count for venue. Corporate and wrongful-death venue are statutory (Ala. Code §§ 6-3-7, 6-5-410(e)).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"83","t":"Local court rules","s":"All local rules were abolished effective April 14, 1992; no local rules are permitted.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The Appendix of Forms is sufficient under the rules; district-court forms are encouraged.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"Cited as the Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure (Ala. R. Civ. P.).","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"Original effective date six months after adoption (1973); district-court modifications effective January 16, 1977.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"},{"n":"87","t":"Limited-scope representation","s":"Under Rule 1.2(c), Ala. R. Prof. Cond., a lawyer may appear for a limited scope stated in a notice filed and served at or before the start of the proceeding. The lawyer’s role ends without leave of court on filing a notice of completion certifying service on the client. Service on the lawyer is required only for matters within the stated scope.","u":"https://judicial.alabama.gov/library/CivilProcedure"}]});
